# 4. Fetching External Data

Modern web applications frequently communicate with external APIs.

JavaScript provides the **Fetch API** for making HTTP requests.

### Basic Syntax

```javascript
fetch(url)
```

`fetch()` returns a Promise.

---

## 🔹 GET Request

Example:

```javascript
fetch("https://api.example.com/users")
    .then(response => response.json())
    .then(data => {
        console.log(data);
    })
    .catch(error => {
        console.log(error);
    });
```

### Flow

```text
fetch()
   ↓
HTTP Request
   ↓
Server
   ↓
Response
   ↓
response.json()
   ↓
JavaScript Data
```

---

# 🔹 Using async/await with Fetch

This is commonly used in modern JavaScript.

```javascript
async function getUsers() {
    try {
        const response = await fetch(
            "https://api.example.com/users"
        );

        const data = await response.json();

        console.log(data);
    } catch (error) {
        console.log(error);
    }
}

getUsers();
```

---

## ⚠️ Checking HTTP Errors

A useful pattern is to check `response.ok`.

```javascript
async function getUsers() {
    try {
        const response = await fetch(
            "https://api.example.com/users"
        );

        if (!response.ok) {
            throw new Error("Failed to fetch users");
        }

        const data = await response.json();

        console.log(data);
    } catch (error) {
        console.error(error.message);
    }
}
```

### Why check `response.ok`?

`fetch()` generally rejects for network-level failures, but HTTP error responses such as `404` or `500` do not automatically reject the Promise.

Therefore:

```javascript
if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error("Request failed");
}
```

is useful when you want HTTP failures to enter your `catch` block.

---

# 🔹 Fetching Data from a Real API

For learning purposes, you can use a public test API such as:

```javascript
async function getPosts() {
    try {
        const response = await fetch(
            "https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts"
        );

        if (!response.ok) {
            throw new Error("Failed to fetch posts");
        }

        const posts = await response.json();

        console.log(posts);
    } catch (error) {
        console.error(error);
    }
}

getPosts();
```

The returned data is typically an array of objects.

Example structure:

```javascript
[
    {
        id: 1,
        title: "...",
        body: "..."
    },
    {
        id: 2,
        title: "...",
        body: "..."
    }
]
```

