# 5. Handling Asynchronous Workflows

Asynchronous workflows often involve multiple stages.

A typical API workflow looks like:

```text
       Start
         ↓
   Send API Request
         ↓
      Loading
      ↙     ↘
 Success     Error
   ↓           ↓
Process       Handle
 Data         Error
   ↓
Update UI
   ↓
   End
```

---

## 🔹 Loading State

While waiting for data:

```jsx
if (loading) {
    return <p>Loading...</p>;
}
```

This gives the user feedback that the request is still running.

---

## 🔹 Success State

When data is successfully received:

```javascript
setUsers(data);
```

Then React re-renders the component using the new state.

---

## 🔹 Error State

If the request fails:

```javascript
setError(error.message);
```

Then display an appropriate message:

```jsx
if (error) {
    return <p>Error: {error}</p>;
}
```

---

## 🔹 Finally / Cleanup State

The `finally` block runs after success or failure.

```javascript
try {
    // Request
} catch (error) {
    // Handle error
} finally {
    setLoading(false);
}
```

This is useful for turning off loading indicators.

---

# 🔄 Sequential Asynchronous Operations

Sometimes one operation depends on the result of another.

```javascript
async function loadData() {
    try {
        const userResponse = await fetch(
            "https://api.example.com/user"
        );

        const user = await userResponse.json();

        const postsResponse = await fetch(
            `https://api.example.com/users/${user.id}/posts`
        );

        const posts = await postsResponse.json();

        console.log(user);
        console.log(posts);
    } catch (error) {
        console.error(error);
    }
}
```

### Flow

```text
Fetch User
    ↓
Get User ID
    ↓
Fetch Posts
    ↓
Process Posts
```

Here the second request depends on data from the first request.

---

# ⚡ Parallel Asynchronous Operations

If multiple independent requests do not depend on each other, they can often run concurrently.

Instead of:

```javascript
const users = await getUsers();
const products = await getProducts();
```

you can use:

```javascript
const [users, products] = await Promise.all([
    getUsers(),
    getProducts()
]);
```

### Flow

```text
          ┌──→ getUsers()
Start ────┤
          └──→ getProducts()
                 │
                 ▼
           Promise.all()
                 │
                 ▼
        Continue when both finish
```

This can reduce unnecessary waiting when the operations are independent.

---

# 🧠 Promise.all()

`Promise.all()` waits for multiple Promises to fulfill.

```javascript
const promise1 = Promise.resolve("A");
const promise2 = Promise.resolve("B");
const promise3 = Promise.resolve("C");

const results = await Promise.all([
    promise1,
    promise2,
    promise3
]);

console.log(results);
```

Output:

```text
["A", "B", "C"]
```

If one Promise rejects, `Promise.all()` rejects.

---

# 🔥 Common Async Pattern

A very common structure is:

```javascript
async function fetchData() {
    try {
        setLoading(true);

        const response = await fetch(url);

        if (!response.ok) {
            throw new Error("Request failed");
        }

        const data = await response.json();

        setData(data);
    } catch (error) {
        setError(error.message);
    } finally {
        setLoading(false);
    }
}
```

### Pattern

```text
try
 │
 ├── Start loading
 ├── Fetch data
 ├── Check response
 ├── Parse data
 └── Update state
 │
catch
 │
 └── Handle error
 │
finally
 │
 └── Stop loading
```

---

# ⚛️ Async Data Flow in React

A typical React API workflow looks like this:

```text
             Component
                 │
                 ▼
            useEffect()
                 │
                 ▼
          Fetch API Data
                 │
          ┌──────┴──────┐
          ▼             ▼
       Success         Error
          │             │
          ▼             ▼
      setData()      setError()
          │
          ▼
       Re-render
          │
          ▼
